connection           REQUIRED  [NESTED, HIGH]
  JDBC connection
  url                  REQUIRED  [STRING, HIGH]
    JDBC URL — jdbc:<dialect>://... (the dialect's provider must be installed)
  user                 REQUIRED  [STRING, HIGH]
    DB user
  password_env         REQUIRED  [STRING, HIGH]
    Name of the DB password: a hub environment variable, else an lm secret
  dialect              optional  [STRING, LOW]
    JDBC dialect (inferred from url scheme if absent)
  zone                 optional  [STRING, LOW]
    Time zone the source writes its dates-and-times without a zone in, for example Europe/Paris (read as UTC if absent)
  password             optional  [STRING, LOW]
    Plain-text DB password (dev/test only — wins over password_env; prefer password_env)
defaults             optional  [NESTED, LOW]
  Per-table defaults
  mode                 optional  [STRING, HIGH]
    Ingest mode (here or in defaults)
    one of: watermark, full
  schema               optional  [STRING, HIGH]
    Table schema (here or in defaults)
  watermark_column     optional  [STRING, MEDIUM]
    The column a watermark read moves forward on
    → Example: watermark_column: updated_at
  pk_columns           optional  [LIST, HIGH]
    Primary key columns (non-empty; here or in defaults)
    → Example: pk_columns: [id]
  indexes              optional  [LIST, LOW]
    Secondary indexes on the target table: [{columns: [...], unique?: bool (default false)}]
    → indexes is a list of { columns: [...], unique?: bool } objects — e.g. indexes: [{ columns: [email] }]
    indexes[]
      columns              REQUIRED  [LIST, HIGH]
        Columns of the index, in order
        → e.g. columns: [email] or [bpartner_id, dateordered]
      unique               optional  [BOOLEAN, LOW]
        Unique index (default false)
        default: false
  rename               optional  [NESTED, LOW]
    Rename source columns before they become hub columns: {source: target}
  refused when given — the runtime never reads these:
    chunk_size           chunk_size is not a knob — jdbc chunking is fixed at 500 rows per read; remove it
      → The engine reads its chunk size from the connector type, never from the manifest
source               REQUIRED  [NESTED, HIGH]
  Source tables
  tables               REQUIRED  [LIST, HIGH]
    List of tables to ingest (at least one)
    → Add a table: { name: 'invoices', mode: 'full', pk_columns: ['id'] }
    tables[]
      name                 REQUIRED  [STRING, HIGH]
        Table name — also names the hub table connector.t_<name>, unless target names it
      target               optional  [STRING, MEDIUM]
        Names the hub table connector.t_<target> in place of the source table's name (#1199)
      mode                 REQUIRED  [STRING, HIGH]
        Ingest mode (here or in defaults)
        one of: watermark, full
      schema               REQUIRED  [STRING, HIGH]
        Table schema (here or in defaults)
      watermark_column     optional  [STRING, MEDIUM]
        The column a watermark read moves forward on
        required when mode = watermark
        → Example: watermark_column: updated_at
      pk_columns           REQUIRED  [LIST, HIGH]
        Primary key columns (non-empty; here or in defaults)
        → Example: pk_columns: [id]
      indexes              optional  [LIST, LOW]
        Secondary indexes on the target table: [{columns: [...], unique?: bool (default false)}]
        → indexes is a list of { columns: [...], unique?: bool } objects — e.g. indexes: [{ columns: [email] }]
        indexes[]
          columns              REQUIRED  [LIST, HIGH]
            Columns of the index, in order
            → e.g. columns: [email] or [bpartner_id, dateordered]
          unique               optional  [BOOLEAN, LOW]
            Unique index (default false)
            default: false
      rename               optional  [NESTED, LOW]
        Rename source columns before they become hub columns: {source: target}
      refused when given — the runtime never reads these:
        chunk_size           chunk_size is not a knob — jdbc chunking is fixed at 500 rows per read; remove it
          → The engine reads its chunk size from the connector type, never from the manifest
schedule             optional  [STRING, MEDIUM]
  Cron expression for recurring ingestion
window               optional  [STRING, MEDIUM]
  Fence scheduled runs to a UTC time window, HH:MM-HH:MM (a manual run bypasses it)
mapping              optional  [NESTED, LOW]
  Not accepted — refused by validation (rows land as the source holds them)
